<title>MEXICO: Mexico to subsidize sorghum sales to 190 pesos/T.</title>
<headline>Mexico to subsidize sorghum sales to 190 pesos/T.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-06-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico plans to subsidize sorghum sales this year to the tune of 170 to 190 pesos a tonne, newspapers reported Tuesday.</p>
<p>Deputy Agriculture Minister Romarico Arroyo Marroquin said the cash support aimed at promoting farmers' sales to livestock producers, would vary in the 170-190 range according to region.</p>
<p>The official announcement has still to be made.</p>
<p>Sales accords for 1.3 million tonnes of sorghum had already been worked out by government authorities, the official added.</p>
<p>Mexico has moved in the last two months to offer farmers subsidies on grains and oilseeds sales to prevent price wars and feed bottlenecks with millers and livestock producers.</p>
<p>((--Chris Aspin, Mexico City newsroom +525 7289530))</p>
